---
title: Slow-cooked bolognese sauce
servings: 10
tags: [italian, beef, pork, lamb, weekend, make-ahead]
cuisine: italian
time: 5 hours 5 minutes
source: The Best Slow-Cooked Bolognese Sauce by J. Kenji López-Alt, Serious Eats, https://www.seriouseats.com/the-best-slow-cooked-bolognese-sauce-recipe
nutrition:
  calories: 865 kcal
---

Set a rack in the lower-middle of the oven and heat it to 300°F (150°C).

Pour the @chicken stock{4%cup} into a #medium bowl{} and sprinkle the @unflavored gelatin{1%oz}(up to 1 1/2 oz) over it. Set aside.

Purée the @whole peeled tomatoes{28%oz}(one can) until smooth. In a narrow cup, purée the @chicken livers{1/2%lb} with a #stick blender{}.

Heat the @olive oil{1/4%cup} in a #large Dutch oven{} over high heat until shimmering. Add the @ground beef{1%lb}(chuck), @ground pork{1%lb}(shoulder) and @ground lamb{1%lb}(shoulder), season with @kosher salt{} and @black pepper{}, and cook ~{10%minutes}, breaking it up, until no pink remains. Take off the heat and stir in the livers.

Meanwhile, cook the @unsalted butter{4%tbsp} and @pancetta{1/2%lb} in a #large skillet{} over medium-high heat ~{8%minutes}, stirring, until the fat has rendered but nothing has browned.

Add the @yellow onion{1}(finely minced), @carrots{2}(finely chopped), @celery{4%ribs}(finely chopped), @garlic{4%cloves}(minced), @fresh sage{1/4%cup}(minced) and half the @parsley{1/2%cup}(minced). Cook ~{8%minutes}, until soft but not coloured, then add to the meat.

Put the pot back over high heat and cook ~{10%minutes}, stirring, until most of the liquid has gone.

Add the @wine{2%cup} and cook until mostly evaporated. Add the stock, the tomatoes, @whole milk{1%cup} and @bay leaves{2}. Season lightly.

Bring to a simmer and move to the oven, uncovered. Cook ~{3%hours} to 4 hours, stirring and scraping the sides now and then, until the liquid has almost gone and a thick layer of fat sits on top. If it is still loose after 4 hours, finish it at a brisk simmer on the stove.

Skim off the fat, leaving about 1 cup in the sauce.

> The sauce can be chilled overnight at this point: lift off the set fat, keep 1 cup of it, and add it back when reheating.

Stir in the @heavy cream{1%cup}, @Parmesan{3%oz}(grated) and @fish sauce{2%tbsp}. Bring to a boil on the stove, stirring hard so it comes together. Season with salt and pepper.

== To serve ==

Heat the sauce in a #large pot{} until just simmering. Boil the @pasta{1%lb}(pappardelle, tagliatelle or penne, dried or fresh) in well-salted water until just short of al dente. Drain, keeping 1/2 cup of the cooking water.

Return the pasta to its pot with enough sauce to coat and a splash of the water. Toss over high heat ~{30%seconds} until the sauce clings. Serve with more @Parmesan{} at the table.

> Keeps a week in the fridge, or freeze it.
